The corruption comes from these lines:

positive := nums[:0]
negative := nums[:0]

In Go, nums[:0] does not allocate a new array. It creates a slice header over the same underlying array as nums, with length 0 and capacity len(nums). Doing that twice makes positive and negative alias the same backing array.

So append(positive, ...) writes into the start of that array, and append(negative, ...) also writes into the start of that same array. Whichever slice appends later can overwrite elements already stored by the other slice. For example, for input []int{1, -1, 2}, after the -1 is appended to negative, index 0 is overwritten, so positive can end up containing -1 instead of 1.

The fix is to allocate separate slices for the two results.
