The main problem is that `max_val` is being used to detect the current streak. It is not the current streak’s value; it is the best value found so far. A streak should continue only when the current element is equal to the previous element.

For `[2, 2, 1, 1, 1]`:

- After seeing `2, 2`, `max_val` is still `2`.
- The later `1`s are compared to `max_val`, so they are never accumulated as a streak.
- Additionally, the final streak is never checked after the loop, so a longest streak ending at the end of the input can be ignored.

So the two key fixes are:

1. Compare `data[i]` to `data[i - 1]` to track consecutive equal values.
2. After the loop, check whether the final streak is the longest.

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn longest_streak(data: &Vec<i32>) -> (i32, usize) {
    let mut max_val = data[0];
    let mut max_count: usize = 1;
    let mut current_count: usize = 1;

    for i in 1..data.len() {
        if data[i] == data[i - 1] {
            current_count += 1;
        } else if current_count > max_count {
            max_count = current_count;
            max_val = data[i - 1];
            current_count = 1;
        } else {
            current_count = 1;
        }
    }

    if current_count > max_count {
        max_count = current_count;
        max_val = data[data.len() - 1];
    }

    (max_val, max_count)
}
</solution>

